<resources>
    <bool name="config_haveHigherAspectRatioScreen">true</bool>

    <!-- Defines the actions shown in advanced reboot submenu -->
    <string-array name="config_restartActionsList">
        <item>restart</item>
        <item>restart_recovery</item>
        <item>restart_download</item>
    </string-array>

    <!-- Whether device has a notch -->
    <bool name="config_haveNotch">true</bool>

    <!--
         Battery charge control.

         The whole mechanism is already present and working on this device; it
         merely ships disabled. Verified on device before changing anything:

           dumpsys lineagehealth
             ChargingControlController Configuration:
               Enabled: false   Mode: 1   Limit: 80
             Provider: ...health.ccprovider.Toggle

         and the path below that is live end to end:

           ChargingControlSettings -> HealthInterface
             -> binder "lineagehealth" (HealthInterfaceService)
             -> ChargingControlController -> Toggle.onBatteryChanged()
             -> binder vendor.lineage.health.IChargingControl/default
             -> ChargingControl::setChargingEnabled()
             -> WriteStringToFile("1", "/sys/class/power_supply/battery/batt_slate_mode")
             -> sec_battery.c: slate_mode -> SEC_BAT_CHG_MODE_BUCK_OFF

         so the kernel node the HAL writes does exist here (batt_slate_mode, 0664,
         u:object_r:sysfs_battery_writable:s0), and the HAL process runs and is
         VINTF-registered. Enabling it therefore needs no kernel change at all.
    -->
    <bool name="config_chargingControlEnabled">true</bool>

    <!--
         Stop charging at the limit, and restart at (limit - margin).

         The stock margin is 10, which on this Toggle provider means the pack
         oscillates between the limit and ten percent below it: 80% -> 70% -> 80%
         indefinitely. That is not hypothetical - it is upstream LineageOS issue
         8115, reported on guacamole/lineage-21, and it is the default behaviour
         here. A margin of 3 keeps the same protection (the pack is never charged
         past the limit) while cutting the visible swing to three points.
    -->
    <integer name="config_chargingControlBatteryRechargeMargin">3</integer>

</resources>
